ing can go to c6. But c6 is a square that might help the Black King escape. However, with the pawn on a7, it\'s still the priority to promote.\n\nBut given that promoting the pawn leads to a Queen, which decides the game, that\'s more important.\n\nSo the best move is P: a6-a7.', 'output': "Situation: White has a bishop on c6, pawns on a6, f2, and g2, and a king on g1. Black's king is exposed on c5 with no other visible pieces. The critical opportunity is advancing the a6 pawn toward promotion (a7 is one step from queening), while Black's king is too distant to stop it immediately. Other moves like bishop maneuvers or kingside pawn pushes are less urgent compared to forcing a queen promotion.\n\nEvaluation: \n- **P: a6-a7** immediately advances the pawn to the 7th rank, one move away from queening. Black's king (c5) requires two moves to reach a7 (e.g., c5→b6→a7), but White can promote next turn (a7→a8=Q), securing a decisive queen. \n- Bishop moves (e.g., **B: c6-d5**) restrict Black's king slightly but cannot force checkmate alone. \n- Kingside pawn pushes (**P: f2-f4**, **P: g2-g3**, etc.) are slow and irrelevant to the immediate promotion threat. \n- King moves (**K: g1-h2**, etc.) waste tempo when the pawn race is critical.\n\nReason: Promoting the a-pawn is the fastest path to an unstoppable advantage. Black cannot prevent queening, and a new queen will easily coordinate with White's king to checkmate. Delaying promotion risks allowing Black's king to interfere, though unlikely here. All other moves neglect this concrete winning plan.\n\nAnswer: P: a6-a7"}
