\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[B: e6-d5, B: e6-c4, B: e6-b3, B: e6-a2, B: e6-d7, B: e6-c8, B: e6-f7, B: e6-g8, K: f2-e1, K: f2-e2, K: f2-e3, K: f2-f1, K: f2-f3, K: f2-g1, K: f2-g2, K: f2-g3, P: f5-f6, P: g4-g5]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': 'Okay, let\'s see. I\'m playing as White, and I need to figure out the best move from the given options. Let me first understand the current board state.\n\nLooking at the board representation:\n\n- There\'s a Black Bishop (BWF1) on e6. Wait, no, the color is part of the piece encoding. Wait, the encoding is [Type][Color][2Step][Moved]. So BWF1 would be Bishop, White, F (didn\'t move 2 steps), 1 (has moved). Wait, but the board shows e6 as BWF1. Wait, but the user said "Current state representation is as follows" and the board has entries like BWF1. Wait, but in the example given earlier, BWF1 is a White Bishop that has moved (since Moved is 1). Wait, but in the problem statement, the board shows e6 as BWF1. Wait, but the user is White, so the pieces on the board: e6 is a White Bishop? Wait, but the Black side is on top (ranks 7-8), White on bottom (1-2). Wait, but e6 is rank 6, which is closer to Black\'s side. Hmm.\n\nWait, let\'s parse the board again.\n\nThe board is shown with rows 8 (top) to 1 (bottom). So row 8 is the top, where Black\'s pieces usually start, but in this case, the board seems almost empty.\n\nLooking at the board:\n\nRow 8: all empty.\n\nRow 7: all empty.\n\nRow 6: e6 has BWF1. So that\'s a White Bishop (since color is W), which has moved (M